{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve","id":1357950,"title":"Learning Curve","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-12-30T21:42:11-08:00","id":1357950,"title":"Learning Curve","content":"I am brand new to this game, but I am a veteran gamer (20th level dungeon master). I have most of the material on PdF, and I dropped the dime to get the core books (gotta have hard copy for a live game). I purchased both 1st and 2nd edition (found a good bargain on the interweb). In a different thread I had asked advice on which edition to start with, Overwhelmingly Awakening2 was recommended, and I agree that it looks much nicer and has some great unique features.<br /> But I gotta say, this is the most complicated game system I have ever delved into.<br /> The reasons I decided to <i>also</i> snag 1st ed are two fold. My first book is actually the Mage Translation guide, which heavily refers to 1st ed. The second reason is the free availlability (on drive thru RPG) of the demo scenarious. My thinking is that a few one shots will help me and a few others learn how to play this game.<br /> As I said, I have them both on PdF already. I have read 2ns up through CharGen, then the same with 1st to note any major differences. So far so good.<br /> Then I get to the Magic section. That is where it gets much harder. More complicated than it needs to be. Looking at 1st, it just seems sooo much simpler.<br /> <br /> I need advice on getting though this learning curve. I would love to hear your anecdotes of how you first approached the game and how it evolved to the way you approach it now.<br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-30T22:36:04-08:00","id":1357956,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve#post1357950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then I get to the Magic section. That is where it gets much harder. More complicated than it needs to be. Looking at 1st, it just seems sooo much simpler.</div> </div> </div> </div>First Edition Awakening builds its magic system under the assumption that most spells default to instant casting and that rotes run on a near-completely different dicepool than improvised spells.<br /> <br /> This begets a problem when those are the only two types of spell and extended casting doesn't actually make a spell any harder to accomplish beyond time constraints, a Willpower surcharge, and a minor material component that a lot of people tend to forget about; further, there's a bunch of different bonuses floating around with no unifying categorization, most of them are rather small, and working off of the same &quot;more rolled successes means a more effective power&quot; as its contemporary gamelines means there's only a minor statistical advantage to instant-casting with pre-loaded spell factors at any level (giving you even less reason to make your spellcasting look like spellcasting when you don't even need your hands free to construct an Imago), plus the need for extra dots in Arcana to use the Advanced tables puts a bit of an arbitrary lock on certain applications of basic Practices.<br /> <br /> Second Edition Awakening makes all spellcasting start from the same pool of Gnosis + Arcanum, defaults casting time to the ritual interval while always giving you at least enough resources to instant-cast any spell you have the Arcana for, organizes the various bonuses such that using a mirror as a ritual implement explicitly needs to be bent toward an effect that makes sense for a mirror to benefit, and gives you more incentive to use those bonuses (by reducing the success-based variability of casting to the normal single-success roll result options) while providing you free steps on the tables through primary factors.<br /> <br /> First Edition's casting is fine if you just want to produce effects, but Second Edition's casting is structured to incentivize mages to act like mages. Where is the excess complication?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T00:03:45-08:00","id":1357966,"title":"","content":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> You misunderstand me. I am not trying to debate merits and versions. I am trying to figure out how to play the game. Everything you just wrote indicates that you understand the game, and that the 2nd ed version works much better for you. Which is great. I get that. My inquiry is into <i>how</i> you achieved that understanding to begin with. The &quot;excess complication&quot; has nothing to do with the rules. It is all about how the information is presented. For example, I am an avid player of the game <i>Ars Magica</i>. Which is also a very complex magic system. Having played through editions, I understand it like the back of my hand. But I feel that the current 5th edition of that game may be a bit confusing for someone who has never played an earlier edition. That is the conclusion I am coming to about <i>Awakening</i>.<br /> <br /> I also understand that intricate crunch helps you to <i>feel</i> like a wizard. It fuels the imagination. <i>Ars Magica</i> does the same thing. But the statement &quot;incentivize mages to act like mages&quot; kinda makes me roll my eyes. How do mages act? It depends on who is writing the story/game/book/movie. It is pure fiction. There is no such thing as magic. In the real world, those who claim to be &quot;mages&quot; or wizards or witches or whatever; they act as eclectic and esoteric as is needed to get people to believe that there is something supernatural about them. It is part of their scam. I don't want &quot;real&quot; mages. I want mages of esoteric fantasy and occult horror. Any rules system can provide that if the players put in the effort.<br /> <br /> I *do* appreciate crunchy rules. But a game is not made better or worse based upon complexity alone. And my struggle is not about the rules. It is about the instructions as written, and the complexity of the manner of instruction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T00:51:15-08:00","id":1357968,"title":"","content":"So, I got in Mage with 1e, liked it a lot. I actually found 2e a lot easier to grok though. Let's put it this way, in 1e, if I tried to do creative thaumaturgy it required a lot of negotiation with the ST, and consulting similar examples in the books in order to try to figure out if I could do what I wanted to do at all. If you could do -anything- original was a bit of a toss up. While 2e mechanics for spellcasting may require some learning it leaves the player with a much surer footing on what they can do. That's my system vs. system take.<br /> <br /> Now, as for dealing with the mechanical complexity of 2e spellcasting, there's a quick spell reference guide right in the book, and there's also at least one web tool out there to help you with making your spells which lowers the mental burden on the ST and the player considerably. The basic uses of Reach are not hard to memorize before very long. The main thing I've seen people actually trip themselves on is thinking that they can spend Reach or Mana to gain dice to their roll.<br /> <br /> If you want to see some folks trying to figure out the system from scratch you can check out Occultists Anonymous on youtube or podcast. There's the occasional blunder, but they get it pretty quick.<br /> <br /> System mastery takes time, but for 2e it's well worth it, and while it may look daunting it's not really that bad. I'd say...maybe 10 sessions tops before your players are comfortable? Probably sooner. Even if they aren't comfortable with making up their own spells, they'll at least be able to grasp how the spells in the book works, and be able to rely on those. And honestly? 2e core has most of the spells you may ever need for a game already in it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T01:46:43-08:00","id":1357976,"title":"","content":"Well the simplest part to remember is that where games like D&amp;D have pre-gen spells, Mage really doesn't. So the first part of spellcasting is asking what you want the spell to do, and not feeling that it needs to be bound by any kind of D&amp;Desque spell design. Mages can do literally ANYTHING, and the majority of their spellcasting is often completely unique to them. Once you know what you want, you can then sort it by Arcanum, and then by Practice within the Arcana to gauge relative power level of the base effect. From there, factors can apply to tweak it into exactly what you want. In all of this, the main point is: YOU as player decide what the spell is capable of, and what costs are going to be paid in order to make it happen just the way you want.<br /> <br /> A good way to do this is to encourage your players to do a little of the 'down and dirty spellcasting' on the fly, getting them to think outside the box and create crude spell ideas on the spot. Get them thinking about what the Arcana and Practices they know can actually DO, and then encourage them to use those notions to spin off wild and wacky magical solutions as they go; the more outside the box and innovative, the better. This will help the players to see the open casting system as a free-for-all in their favour, and with your encouragement, you can then get them evaluating the performance of their ideas and start them thinking about solid Praxes based on these. If you want to control the power level for new players, you might like to limit their ability to opt for excessive Reach and suffer Paradox until you and they have a better grip on the system's mechanics. Maybe start with all spells set to default, and then gradually introduce concepts like Reach and sacrificing dice to increase spell factors, with the cost of Paradox only biting once this step is taken.<br /> <br /> Another way you can go with more experienced players is to try dry-designing some spells that suit the personality of your character, and keeping these as a kind of default 'useful spell' list for smooth play. You can figure out exactly what those special personal spells are meant to do, and then tweak them in play to make them fit the moment's need. In play, this is basically what Praxes are: spells that your character has trained to excellence in, which may be and often are spells of their own design. When you start play, those Praxes are likely going to be chosen from the nice easy list provided in the Mage rules. But it's much better if they aren't, and you design them as unique spells for your character from the off. Even if you don't, designing a few as you go and adding them as Praxes to gradually replace the stock-standard spells in the rulebook is really the way to go. It'll give you practice at spell design and the ability to compare what you've designed to the needs the character has faced, and design better from there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2992-crapcarp","name":"crapcarp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2992_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T05:55:43-08:00","id":1357992,"title":"","content":"Mages are specialised in information-gathering. A murder mystery that would take an entire session in D&amp;D could be solved within minutes by a beginning Mage. The meat of the game comes after tat. It's best to present your players with open-ended challenges and dilemmas, with each approach having its pros and cons. Your players will have to navigate between:<br /> <br /> Their Obsessions and Aspirations<br /> Wisdom and ethical conduct<br /> Order responsibilities and politics<br /> Effort or path of least resistance<br /> Acting carefully or responding quickly<br /> <br /> This may lead to long discussions between players. This is fine, but be ready to push things along if things go out of hand. Once they pursue a course of action, try to figure out the most likely outcomes. In Mage, it's particulary important to let go of your expectations where the plot will go. Try to figure out what the motivation of NPCs are, or how supernatural phenomena work, and have them respond to PCs actions accordingly. If your players gathered all the necessary information and thought it through, they will probably get what they wanted, but that rarely happens. There's often some missing information, PC being flawed human beings, time constraints or needing to juggle multiple problems at the same time.<br /> <br /> Here's an old post of mine, with some useful links.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Teatime</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1277456-storytelling-advice-how-to-create-challenge-in-mage?p=1277478#post1277478\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If your players are new to Mage, perhaps presenting them with simpler challenges is the right thing to do - at least at the beginning. They might need to practise their magical problem solving skills before you throw impossible tasks at them. Then, as they get into the groove, you gradually increase the difficulty level.<br /> <br /> From a different angle, instead of presenting them with puzzles to solve, you might present them with dillemas:<br /> <a href=\"http://goblinpunch.blogspot.com/2015/07/the-5-types-of-ethical-dilemmas.html\" target=\"_blank\">Link</a><br /> <a href=\"https://mythcreants.com/blog/five-dualities-that-can-replace-good-and-evil/\" target=\"_blank\">Link</a><br /> <a href=\"https://mythcreants.com/blog/five-more-dualities-that-can-replace-good-and-evil/\" target=\"_blank\">Link</a><br /> <br /> These choices are more obvious, which might put your players back in their comfort zone. Advanced problem solving is not necessary to progress, but if they exhibit some they can find a third, better solution. And even if they don't, their choices still matter.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's also this valuable thread over at RPG.net. The site seems to be down at the moment, so I'm linking the Google cache:<br /> <a href=\"http://webcache.googleusercontent.com/search?q=cache:uD-Ou_BVEHwJ:https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/mage-the-awakening-2e-seeking-advice-running-mage-for-the-first-time.815371/&amp;hl=pl&amp;gl=pl&amp;strip=1&amp;vwsrc=0\" target=\"_blank\">Link</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T08:46:16-08:00","id":1358013,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1357966#post1357966\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I feel that the current 5th edition of that game may be a bit confusing for someone who has never played an earlier edition. That is the conclusion I am coming to about <i>Awakening</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Then that is not &quot;more complicated than it needs to be.&quot; That is &quot;more complicated than you can take in as a new player.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the statement &quot;incentivize mages to act like mages&quot; kinda makes me roll my eyes. How do mages act? It depends on who is writing the story/game/book/movie. It is pure fiction. There is no such thing as magic. In the real world, those who claim to be &quot;mages&quot; or wizards or witches or whatever; they act as eclectic and esoteric as is needed to get people to believe that there is something supernatural about them. It is part of their scam. I don't want &quot;real&quot; mages. I want mages of esoteric fantasy and occult horror. Any rules system can provide that if the players put in the effort. </div> </div> </div>And my point is that a system where you have a reason to make use of mystically-significant items, locations, knowledge, and resources besides &quot;I can add an extra die to my moderately-sized dicepool, which I don't really have to reduce to accomplish the things I want to accomplish with magic&quot; does better at making characters who engage with those parts of the setting specific to mages in Mage: the Awakening than a system where that thing I just said is the bulk of the commonly-required interaction with a magic system where casting can be accomplished while blindfolded, bound, and gagged without especial difficulty.<br /> <br /> The word &quot;mage&quot; does not exist in a vacuum and it especially doesn't do so in a fictional setting where magic is real and the things called mages have anything to do with the charlatans you know of in the real world. Mage provides information on how and how well certain things can benefit your mage character's magic so that you will be incentivized to make use of them. To what end do you think telling someone that something they are saying makes you roll your eyes incentivizes them and their peers to continue trying to help you?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I *do* appreciate crunchy rules. But a game is not made better or worse based upon complexity alone. And my struggle is not about the rules. It is about the instructions as written, and the complexity of the manner of instruction. </div> </div> </div>And my goal in asking where the excess complication is is to find points to smooth your particular learning curve. I cannot do that without specific information beyond throwing out educated guesses based on other people's experiences, as others have done above.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T09:00:45-08:00","id":1358018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1357966#post1357966\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also understand that intricate crunch helps you to <i>feel</i> like a wizard. It fuels the imagination. <i>Ars Magica</i> does the same thing. But the statement &quot;incentivize mages to act like mages&quot; kinda makes me roll my eyes. How do mages act? It depends on who is writing the story/game/book/movie. It is pure fiction. There is no such thing as magic. In the real world, those who claim to be &quot;mages&quot; or wizards or witches or whatever; they act as eclectic and esoteric as is needed to get people to believe that there is something supernatural about them. It is part of their scam. I don't want &quot;real&quot; mages. I want mages of esoteric fantasy and occult horror. Any rules system can provide that if the players put in the effort.</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically your average mage is encouraged to use magic and, in doing so, 'walk the tightrope between Gnosis and Wisdom.' Not like Darrin in Bewitched, you have magic, it makes life easier, but it's also easy to suddenly find your day is full of animal people because you took an idea and ran with it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I *do* appreciate crunchy rules. But a game is not made better or worse based upon complexity alone. And my struggle is not about the rules. It is about the instructions as written, and the complexity of the manner of instruction. </div> </div> </div>The rules are pretty easy when you get used to them, spellcasting especially. There's an appendix in the back that has all of the rules for it shaved down to an easy process. If you aren't used to the specific arcana and the capabilities of each dot in them (called a practice) there's a section for that and for the most part they work in parallel. Doing easy Lethal damage is at the same dot in every Arcana, just like doing Aggravated, or turning yourself into a representative of your Arcana.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T09:46:37-08:00","id":1358025,"title":"","content":"In my experience 2e spellcasting is only &quot;more complicated&quot; if you only use spells from the respective books, and even then not significantly more complicated.<br /> <br /> Going off-book (which you're supposed to be able to do) is an absolute nightmare in 1e due to how horribly inconsistent everything is.<ul><li>Is a spell Vulgar or Covert? This comes from Ascension and has no tie-in to the metaphysics of Awakening, making it both lore-breaking and difficult to apply. It is simply not an issue in 2e.</li> <li>What Arcana are needed to cast the spell? Again, Awakening 1e uses an Ascension based system for this that makes no sense given the lore of Awakening. 2e doesn't have this problem. There's also the &quot;speed-bumps&quot; in 1e that simply make no sense, why is fire harder to control than an animal (the former requires Forces 3, the latter Life 2).</li> <li>What is the spell going to <i>do</i>? When I played 1e spells rarely outright failed, but far too often you didn't accrue enough successes for the spell to do what you wanted it to do, or you had to spend time figuring out how to piece together your successes into something useful. Other gamelines solve this by having their powers be fairly limited with relatively clear scaling. In 2e you construct the spell first and then see if you manage to cast it, leading to far fewer &quot;feel bad&quot; moments and ultimately faster gameplay.</li> </ul><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1452235-proin-s-legacy-hub\" target=\"_blank\">proin's Legacy hub</a>","edited":{"userid":2757,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","user":"proindrakenzol","date":". 12-31-2019, 03:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","name":"proindrakenzol","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1514"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T12:33:45-08:00","id":1358051,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1358013#post1358013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To what end do you think telling someone that something they are saying makes you roll your eyes incentivizes them and their peers to continue trying to help you?.</div> </div> </div> </div>It seems to have worked rather well. It incentivized *you* to write a long post full of useful information. It also served as a sort of a filter. I want to talk rules and instructions, not about &quot;real magic&quot;. It is an issue I have encountered in Ascension and Ars Magica before.<br /> <br /> As an aside, I have not played any form of D&amp;D in decades. I keep mentioning Ars Magica because I have written for that game. It is in some ways vastly *more* complicated than Awakening. It is the original &quot;free-form&quot; magic system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And my goal in asking where the excess complication is is to find points to smooth your particular learning curve. I cannot do that without specific information beyond throwing out educated guesses based on other people's experiences, as others have done above. </div> </div> </div>It is not the rules that are too complex. It is the manner of instruction. Not the writing per se, but they layout. Thanks to the advice of a few here, I now know to look for some summary sheets towards the back. I figure the learning curve will get easier when my hard copy arrives in the mail. I can bookmark certain pages and flip back &amp; forth with much more ease.<br /> In the meantime, stories of experiences and educated guesses are very helpful. Pointing me towards demo scenarios would also be helpful. The only ones I have found are for 1st ed. Examples of play are also helpful.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1357968#post1357968\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you want to see some folks trying to figure out the system from scratch you can check out Occultists Anonymous on youtube or podcast. There's the occasional blunder, but they get it pretty quick.</div> </div> </div> </div>Awesome! That is exactlly the sort of thing I am seeking. I thank you very much and will be checking that out.<br /> And some of the links someone else posted. Stuff like that is also helpful.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T13:18:59-08:00","id":1358061,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1358051#post1358051\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems to have worked rather well. It incentivized *you* to write a long post full of useful information.</div> </div> </div> </div>I told you the same things I told you in my first post and you're now admitting to essentially trolling, which gets flagged and is not a fantastic reason to continue trying to help you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It also served as a sort of a filter. I want to talk rules and instructions, not about &quot;real magic&quot;. It is an issue I have encountered in Ascension and Ars Magica before. </div> </div> </div>I am not talking about real magic, <i>was not</i> talking about real magic, and have no interest in talking about real magic in this thread on a forum for a fictional game. I am not saying that &quot;intricate crunch&quot; helps me to &quot;feel like a wizard.&quot; &quot;Mages acting like mages&quot; means &quot;your mage characters do things to perform magic that are discernible in the fiction as something other than 'and then a miracle occurs.'&quot; This is not difficult to understand.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It is not the rules that are too complex. It is the manner of instruction. Not the writing per se, but they layout. </div> </div> </div>The layout is clear and presented in a straightforward order at the front of the section or in the beginning of its most relevant Arcanum, as opposed to scattered throughout the examples, placed at the end of the list of examples, or inferred from examples across several books published months and years after the basic system's release. My question remains: &quot;Where is the excess complication?&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-31T15:28:38-08:00","id":1358093,"title":"","content":"Satchel: I seek no conflict with you. It is perhaps best that we ignore each other. I am sorry I offended you.<br /> <br /> Everyone else: I am grateful for your anecdotes and advice. Keep them coming! It is very helpful and encouraging.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-01T21:08:05-08:00","id":1358346,"title":"","content":"Reading and rereading things a bit more, it is starting to come into focus. I still have issues with the order of information. There are a lot of technical jargon terms with no definition. I do not mean new or unique words such as Yantra or such. Those are (vaguely) defined in the Lexicon. I mean normal words used in a technical way that are only defined by reading the text twice. For example, I know what they mean by &quot;Reach&quot; now. But the rules mention Reach long before it tells you what Reach does. I intend to write up an &quot;Idiot's Glossary&quot;, which will both help me memorize the way terms are used and will assist me in teaching others how to play the game.<br /> <br /> The Occultists Anonymous you-tube was very very helpful! It helped to clarify what I just read and made rereading it much more enlightening. Are there any other resources such as this that anyone knows of? Maybe a PbP site with an active game I can read for examples.<br /> <br /> There is still a lot to digest, but I am getting there. And I can use all the advice I can get. Please no criticizing me for being confused or angry defense of the game. I find it confusing, that is just the reality. Maybe I am stupid. Please don't harass me for my idiocy.<br /> <br /> The advice I currently seek runs along the lines of &quot;you will want to house rule (x)\u201d, \u201cmost new Storytellers/players forget about (y) rule\u201d or \u201cdon\u2019t worry about (z) until you\u2019re a few sessions in\u201d.<br /> <br /> I am grateful for any kindness and assistance.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-01T22:52:46-08:00","id":1358356,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1357966#post1357966\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I *do* appreciate crunchy rules. But a game is not made better or worse based upon complexity alone. And my struggle is not about the rules. It is about the instructions as written, and the complexity of the manner of instruction.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am not familiar with MtA, but have read the CoD and DtD manuals. I also find that the way the rules are presented makes them hard (for me) to understand in a unified way. What I really want to see are carefully written examples of the crunch. I had to go through many iterations to produce a correct example of demon/angel combat and needed help from many forum members to debug it. It really helped me better understand the rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24502-kieranmullen","name":"KieranMullen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-03T16:20:22-08:00","id":1358727,"title":"","content":"I'd probably drop Rotes and Praxis to start with: Partially added complexity, partially choosing the right ones is a weird bit of game finesse and I have no idea how a completely new player is supposed to pick these well. Whereas choosing them after having played a few sessions means you are more likely to get them right. <br /> <br /> The first sessions spellcasting is going to be slow, and it will take a while to get used to the absurd versatility of mage spells. Take the time, go through it slowly and don't worry if you have realised you have made mistakes later. <br /> The Summary Sheets are your friend.<br /> <br /> Paradox is another thing you could bring in slightly later, after all you can have them cast a few spells before dangling the forbidden fruit of Bonus Reaches (I wouldn't wait to long on this but you can avoid it for a few spells.) <br /> <br /> Running some sort of prelude session, my first Mage game eased the players into casting, we had basically to wizard school settings, where we were taken though spell casting etc. Start a little more structured, get players used to their toolkit before releasing them fully into the wild.<br /> <br /> The Advantage of the Mage system (both of them) is that to a degree it's a steep learning curve but it gets easier at the top, because there is so much pattern to the system. I can now use either far more fluidly than DnD for instance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/711-mcgonigle","name":"mcgonigle","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-03T22:09:43-08:00","id":1358766,"title":"","content":"Have you given the Appendix that gives spellcasting rules a simplified step by step a look?<br /> <br /> As far as I can see, Mage 2e is a lot easier to follow than 1e was for many.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":8331,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","user":"milo v3","date":". 01-04-2020, 01:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-04T01:25:55-08:00","id":1358786,"title":"","content":"This might be of some use:<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.voidstate.com/rpg/mage-spell-helper/#/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.voidstate.com/rpg/mage-spell-helper/#/\" target=\"_blank\"> Improvised Spell Helper for Mage 2nd Edition </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.voidstate.com/rpg/mage-spell-helper/#/\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.voidstate.com/rpg/mage-spell-helper/#/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Maybe play with that a bit. It has a lot of stuff that's strewn through the magic chapter in one place, and it does some of the work for you. It might not be practical for play though.<br /> <br /> Something that helped me was to write down Reach, factor, and Mana expenditures as I built spells during sessions, especially for spells my characters used the same way a lot of the time. Kind of lay out the base line of what the spell usually does and how many dice I get for it, and then factor in incidentals depending on the circumstances. For example, if I use an invisibility spell on myself a lot, I just have to subtract X number of dice or spend a Reach to include other people without having to build the whole thing from the ground up every time.<br /> <br /> One thing I find a lot of people forget is that spells cast at ritual length by default, and that you've always got to spend a Reach to do things instantly. It's come up a few times at tables I've played at.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-05T11:17:41-08:00","id":1359101,"title":"","content":"This is a fantastic resource! I thank you very much <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-06T16:50:45-08:00","id":1359377,"title":"","content":"The <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/877640-creative-thaumaturgy-fallen-world-edition\">Creative Thaumaturgy</a> thread in this forum is also very useful as learning/practice. Think up a spell or two, or just pick a couple ideas from the thread. Try designing them, compare your designs with those in the thread. Complex spells often go through a few iterations on the thread. Post your design to the thread, see what people think.<br /> <br /> It's 111 pages long right now, so a bit difficult to manage. If you subscribe to it, in the Advanced Search form you can tick 'only threads I subscribe to' and get more focused search results.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/181-codesmithkarl","name":"codesmithKarl","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-08T16:44:27-08:00","id":1359796,"title":"","content":"I run games for some friends and while we're all old White Wolf vets Mage was our first Chronicles-era setting. We all felt intimidated both by the intricacy of the ruleset and the breadth of the setting. The mage cosmos is huge -- astral realms, lower depths, shadow, underworld, emanations... any of those would be a complete game setting for an RPG, but mage has them all and some of them are essential elements of several Paths. A Mastigos can bring the Astral Realms into a story at any point.<br /> <br /> What we did is take turns running very small &quot;standard format&quot; stories consisting of one social encounter, one fight scene, and one &quot;other thing&quot; from mage. We would run a story, then during the week we would review the rules again in light of what we did during the story and find mistakes we made or ways we could have utilized more of the game system. After around ten little stories we all felt pretty expert and I started running major stories. I still sometimes hit little edges and wrinkles that I missed or misunderstood, or I forget things I used to know and have to relearn them. There are some concepts that the core rulebook just didn't have space to cover to my satisfaction. For example it took me **forever** to &quot;get&quot; spirits; I finally came to understand that as ST I can make my setting such that there are a lot of spirits in Twilight clustered around sources of appropriate Essence and that made them much easier to incorporate for Thrysus in my game. Asking questions on this forum helps a ton, and remember Cunningham's Law: the best way to get the right answer on the internet is not to ask a question; it's to post the wrong answer.<br /> <br /> tl;dr: Running short throw-away stories and doing post mortems on them for &quot;mistakes&quot; is a good way to learn this system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-09T19:04:44-08:00","id":1360024,"title":"","content":"Oh man, I have run campaigns in both Ars Magica and Mage, and Ars is several degrees more complicated than Mage.<br /> <br /> In my opinion If you can get Ars you can get Mage. I know though that unlike Ars a lot of details of the rules are buried in the prose. No convenient formulas in text blocks, so you have to really read the rules. Though there is a summary in the back.<br /> <br /> Maybe if you detailed some of the specific things you are having trouble understanding we can help explain them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12488,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12488-jeremysbrain","user":"Jeremysbrain","date":". 01-09-2020, 10:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12488-jeremysbrain","name":"Jeremysbrain","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-09T22:55:15-08:00","id":1360067,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jeremysbrain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360024#post1360024\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh man, I have run campaigns in both Ars Magica and Mage, and Ars is several degrees more complicated than Mage.<br /> <br /> In my opinion If you can get Ars you can get Mage. I know though that unlike Ars a lot of details of the rules are buried in the prose. No convenient formulas in text blocks, so you have to really read the rules. Though there is a summary in the back.<br /> <br /> Maybe if you detailed some of the specific things you are having trouble understanding we can help explain them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I started with ArM4, the previous edition. My knowledge of that helped me quickly comprehend ArM5, the latest edition. To me it is easy. But after trying to introduce some oWoD players to it, I realized how very complex it can be. But for me, it was a language I already knew, so it was easy to adapt the complexity. I started to come to the same conclusion about <i>Awakening</i>, and on this very thread somebody jumped all over my $#!+.<br /> But I am not so easily discouraged.<br /> I want to learn this game so I can teach it to others and play it. There are a lot of interesting features that intrigue me, some I want to import as HRs in my Ars Magica game.<br /> Features unique to 2nd Awakening. Arcane Experience, Obsessions, Conditions that grant xp for playing them as opposed to Flaws that grant Virtue points, and more. Some features are in both editions, and I kinda want to HR them into an Ascension game. Wisdom most of all.<br /> The most likely approach I will take is an HR hybrid of Ascension &amp; Awakening using the Translation guide.<br /> I am starting to (somewhat) understand the mechanics. It will take practice and repetition. But there are a few lingering questions...<br /> * What's the deal with Attainments? I am thinking some of them just come automatically, and only special Legacy attainments require xp. Or ones you invent. But I have yet to wrap my head around *how* you do it. On paper, not in game. What are the guidelines for creating ne attainments? And one spot says they always cost mana yet another says they never cost mana.I need to reread that.<br /> * Why can't a rote be a praxis? To me, a Rote seems like a Formulaic spell and a Praxis is like a Mastered spell (to use Ars Magica terms). Why cant you Praxis a Rote? Game balance issue? Or something I am missing?<br /> * As for conditions, can a player choose any as part of CharGen? If not, why? If so, then what is the guide or limit for such?<br /> * How do you manage swift resolution at the table? It seems like every spell can potentially halt the game while flipping pages to figure out how many dice to add &amp; subtract. The spell helper link above is a great asset. I am thinking of maybe copying it out as a cheat sheet (once I understand the nuances better).<br /> * How do I approach the supplemental material? There is a lot of it, but it is all for first edition. Is it still compatible?<br /> <br /> There is always going to be more questions. That is just off the top of my head.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-09T23:45:59-08:00","id":1360075,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am starting to (somewhat) understand the mechanics. It will take practice and repetition. But there are a few lingering questions...<br /> * What's the deal with Attainments? I am thinking some of them just come automatically, and only special Legacy attainments require xp. Or ones you invent. But I have yet to wrap my head around *how* you do it. On paper, not in game. What are the guidelines for creating ne attainments? And one spot says they always cost mana yet another says they never cost mana.I need to reread that.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Arcana Attainments are received simply by having the relevant dots in the specific Arcanum. All of the Arcana Attainments require mana to use.<br /> <br /> There's no spot that says Attainments <i>never</i> cost mana (that I can recall), what is true is that Legacy Attainments never cost more than one mana, even if the spell they mimic would cost more than<br /> one.<br /> <br /> Legacy Attainments cost experience to learn. Creating Legacy Attainments based on spells is described in the Legacy creation section (thought it is a tad confusing). I and other forumites have made custom legacies or updated old legacies to 2e, so I'd take a look at those for ideas on your own.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> * Why can't a rote be a praxis? To me, a Rote seems like a Formulaic spell and a Praxis is like a Mastered spell (to use Ars Magica terms). Why cant you Praxis a Rote? Game balance issue? Or something I am missing? </div> </div> </div>Probably mostly game balance. Flavor wise, a Rote is a spell encoded with certain mnemonics by a Master to make it easier for others to cast (reach as if they had five dots in the relevant Arcanum) and the ability to use a Skill as a yantra (potentially a +6 if you have 5 dots in the skill and it's an Order Rote skill); a Praxis is a freeform Imago you've formed so many times it's basically second nature, hence receiving an exceptional on three instead of five successes. Thought process wise they're different, and mechanically getting up to +6 dice from a single yantra plus a reduced exceptional threshold is very strong.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> * As for conditions, can a player choose any as part of CharGen? If not, why? If so, then what is the guide or limit for such? </div> </div> </div>If a persistent condition makes sense for a character at generation, you as the ST can certainly give it to them. For example, if they are playing a wheelchair bound character, giving the player Leg Wrack (Persistent) in both legs makes sense, but I would personally avoid non-persistent conditions at character generation or allowing so many conditions the character can't reasonably be expected to actually be playable. Basically, if you don't think it'll be fun for you, don't let them do it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> * How do you manage swift resolution at the table? It seems like every spell can potentially halt the game while flipping pages to figure out how many dice to add &amp; subtract. The spell helper link above is a great asset. I am thinking of maybe copying it out as a cheat sheet (once I understand the nuances better). </div> </div> </div>Practice. It takes a while until you get fast at throwing the spell together. I usually do reach first to calculate paradox, then factors, but ymmv.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> * How do I approach the supplemental material? There is a lot of it, but it is all for first edition. Is it still compatible? </div> </div> </div><i>Signs of Sorcery</i> is for second edition and is great. The 1e stuff is hit or miss. Things like <i>Sanctum and Sigil</i> andthe various Order books are largely still relevant, but the Legacy books are purely fluff about the specific legacies contained therein.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There is always going to be more questions. That is just off the top of my head. </div> </div> </div>The &quot;Ask a Simple Question&quot; thread can be a good place for quick questions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1452235-proin-s-legacy-hub\" target=\"_blank\">proin's Legacy hub</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","name":"proindrakenzol","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1514"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-10T06:01:10-08:00","id":1360112,"title":"","content":"My only suggestion to you is to approach learning this game the same way you probably learned Ars Magica. Start with mortals. Play some adventures using only mortal characters. This will let you focus on learning the dice mechanic, skill system, conditions, beats and other basic game concepts. I suggest you get the Chronicles of Darkness book for that. Once you feel comfortable with that then introduce mage characters and focus on learning the spell system. Don't worry about supplements for now, just focus on the core Mage book and Chronicles book (you don't actually need the Chronicles book, but I highly recommend you start there).<br /> <br /> Also, I too will recommend reading the Ask a Simple Question thread. Not just the Mage one, but also the one on the Chronicles board.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12488-jeremysbrain","name":"Jeremysbrain","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-11T10:11:14-08:00","id":1360344,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's the deal with Attainments? I am thinking some of them just come automatically, and only special Legacy attainments require xp. Or ones you invent.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, as described in the core, <i>Utility </i>Attainments come automatically. <i>Legacy </i>Attainments, you spend experience for after joining a legacy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What are the guidelines for creating ne attainments?</div> </div> </div> </div>There are no guidelines for creating new Utility Attainments. The 'guidelines' for creating new Legacy Attainments, such as they are, start on page 198 (it's the entire Legacy Attainment section). They aren't very good guidelines, and most of the official example legacies we've seen so far have questionable adherence to them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And one spot says they always cost mana yet another says they never cost mana.</div> </div> </div> </div>Legacy Attainments reduce all Mana costs above 1 to 1. Utility Attainments have no special relationship to Mana - some of them require it to function, some of them allow you to access more powerful effects with it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why can't a rote be a praxis?</div> </div> </div> </div>A Praxis is a spell that you have gained personal insight into, internalizing through use and your own connection to the Supernal<br /> A Rote is a spell that some Master has managed to codify in a format that allows others to cast <i>as if they were that Master</i>.<br /> You can't draw on your internalized mastery of the Supernal while embedding your casting in someone else's mystical identity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for conditions, can a player choose any as part of CharGen?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not a default part of the character creation rules, no. If someone wanted to play a character laboring under a persistent Condition (eg. Blind) I would allow it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If not, why?</div> </div> </div> </div>They chose not to dedicate wordcount to min-maxing via a Flaw-like system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do you manage swift resolution at the table?</div> </div> </div> </div>My players each maintain a 'Grimoire' in Google Docs of fully calculated spells that they frequently use, making minor adjustments as needed for the situation at hand.<br /> There is also a mostly-accurate digital tool available, I believe it was linekd earlier in the thread.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am thinking of maybe copying it out as a cheat sheet (once I understand the nuances better).</div> </div> </div> </div>The spellcasting quick reference at the back of the book has most of the things you'd need to extract for a cheat sheet.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do I approach the supplemental material?</div> </div> </div> </div>Get Signs of Sorcery. It is the only 2E Supplement. Night Horrors - Nameless &amp; Accursed will be the second.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is a lot of it, but it is all for first edition. Is it still compatible?</div> </div> </div> </div>For the 1E material, it's a coin flip. The mechanics are largely not useful unless you want to rewrite the spells for 2E. If you decide to do this, keep in mind that 90% of spells that used to be conjunctional probably don't need to be, and you should utterly disregard the dot level of 1E spells and recalculate a new one based on what the Practice would be in 2E (which is not always what it was in 1E).<br /> <br /> The setting material from supplements is <i>mostly</i> useful, but 1E took years to get its setting straight, so the usefulness of a given product varies. A large setting change in 2E is that 'Atlantis' is de-emphasized, and belief in its existence as a literal place is practically nonexistent in 2E material. <i>Something</i> existed, but 'Atlantis' is an ideal and a metaphor, not history.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-16T02:01:33-08:00","id":1361241,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1360067#post1360067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">* Why can't a rote be a praxis? To me, a Rote seems like a Formulaic spell and a Praxis is like a Mastered spell (to use Ars Magica terms). Why cant you Praxis a Rote? Game balance issue? Or something I am missing?</div> </div> </div> </div>The relationship is more like, once you have the Praxis for a spell, it makes more sense that you can design a Rote of it for others to use.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/181-codesmithkarl","name":"codesmithKarl","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-16T13:08:40-08:00","id":1361331,"title":"","content":"Can you have a praxis and a rote in the same spell?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12488-jeremysbrain","name":"Jeremysbrain","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-16T13:17:25-08:00","id":1361333,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jeremysbrain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1361331#post1361331\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can you have a praxis and a rote in the same spell?</div> </div> </div> </div>You can, but you can only use the effects of one at a time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-28T12:16:57-08:00","id":1363836,"title":"","content":"I would say that the game has a pretty high entry floor. There's a bunch or lore you need to absorb that player characters are assumed to know (you can reduce the load by making characters &quot;freshly awakened&quot; but that introduces some problems of it's own), quite a bit of terminology to absorb and the spellcasting system just requires a bit of dedication to learn how it works. <br /> <br /> Overall it can be hard if your style is for the GM to introduce the players to the game since there's a lot of ground to cover. So I recommend that the players read the rules themselves cause otherwise the magic system doesn't work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2636-menace","name":"Menace","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2636_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"527"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-30T22:41:26-08:00","id":1364287,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Menace</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1357950-learning-curve?p=1363836#post1363836\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would say that the game has a pretty high entry floor. There's a bunch or lore you need to absorb that player characters are assumed to know (you can reduce the load by making characters &quot;freshly awakened&quot; but that introduces some problems of it's own), quite a bit of terminology to absorb and the spellcasting system just requires a bit of dedication to learn how it works.<br /> <br /> Overall it can be hard if your style is for the GM to introduce the players to the game since there's a lot of ground to cover. So I recommend that the players read the rules themselves cause otherwise the magic system doesn't work.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for understanding what I am facing <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> For years all I have played is Ars Magica, online with other Ars Magica fans.It is sooo much easier when everyone knows the rules already and is just as into the game as you are.<br /> Since this past Summer, I have been gaming with a live group in my area that I met on line. The current game is an oWoD mix. I haven't touched any WW games in many years, and it has been fun rediscovering them. But something about that magic system leaves me unsatisfied. I looked at first edition awakening, and back then it didn't grab me. The Atlantis stuff seems a bit new age, and there is no Order of Hermes (again, I am an Ars Magica fanatic).Anyways, I got back into WW games with Sorcerers Crusade. I started researching them, learning rules and looking into new books. I rediscovered Awakening, looked at it with new eyes (I had since gained some experience in design and writing for gaming, Ars Magica again).<br /> Anyways, I now have hard copy of both editions of Awakening. And the Mage Translation guide. This live group, they are stubborn. If I am going to get them to try this game out, I am going to have to do it in baby steps. I have been crafting an HR hybrid. A few of them in fact (my ideas are yet unstable). Start them off with basic rules and a setting they are familiar with, and introduce a few new things at a time.<br /> <br /> The process of doing this is what has helped me learn more than anything. That and the spell calculator web site someone linked in this thread (thanx again!). I now feel comfortable enough with the RAW that I could play in a game (if I knew someone that ran it), but I am not yet ready to both teach and GM the game.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-31T07:02:28-08:00","id":1364338,"title":"","content":"As old <b>MtAw</b> ST introducing many players I will say you simply - start with newly created Awakened - or even start Mortal and run Awakening on first two sessions. After this in-game event give them 2 dots in most important Arcanum, 1 dot in second and 1 dot of third important. Most important Arcanum points to character Path. And make them few sessions of Orders 'magic school' / Harry Potter for grown ups. Really, it<i> will </i>ease the introduction to the game, mechanics and concepts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-31T22:46:52-08:00","id":1364474,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar25651_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"25651\" data-vbnamecard=\"25651\">Marko Markoko</a>, give PCs under Mysterium or Mysterium-related Mentor. Wings of Dragon, as Order, wants to have good relations with any other Pentacle Orders, to have their reputation pull on the later. ( Called Guanxi in their dogma. ) Mysterium is known from teaching basics of magics to newly Awakened and sending them to others, more suitable Orders for candidates - only to ask favors later on for giving candidates to other Orders. Guardians of the Veil makes the same trick, as they found most of new mages at all, from Labirynth, but rest of Pentacle is always suspecting that Guardians learned mages could be spies. And often they are right. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Where Mysterium learned mages just have hang on the magic learning for magic sake. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Mysterium guys are known from knowing <i>a lot</i> of other mages, with whom they exchange apprentices, directly because of Guanxi.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 02-01-2020, 02:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-31T23:11:57-08:00","id":1364478,"title":"","content":"If you want to learn setting lore and understand from where Orders are - may I advise my own <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/387074-history-of-awakened-mage-timeline\"><b><i>History of Awakened</i> - Mage Timeline</b></a> project? It's sum up of whole timeline of gameline, scattered across all the books on Mage, compiled into digestible form. It's mostly based on canon events, but I added here and there non-canon ones, to describe process we do not have data better ( marked where using fan events where). I highly advise to give a read, it clarifies a lot about Orders, they relations, and how they be in-setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}